The Quranic phrase: "Certainly We shall test you with something of fear..." The lam here answers an oath, and its implied form is: "By God, We shall certainly test you." The wisdom of the trial is to make evident the obedient from the disobedient, not so that He may come to know something of which He was not already aware. The scholars differed over who the verse was revealed about: some said it was revealed concerning the Jews, and it was said that it was revealed concerning the Muslims.

"With something of fear": fear of the enemy. "And hunger": through famine and drought. "And loss of wealth": through deficiency and destruction. "And of lives": through sickness, old age and death. "And of fruits": through blights. It was also said: through children, because they are the fruits of the hearts. The wisdom of testing with these things is this: when they endure patiently, then everyone who hears of it after them will know that they endured only because of the truth they had come to recognise.

{And give good tidings to the patient}
